### What is Depth-First Search?

Depth-first search (DFS) is a graph and tree traversal algorithm that follows one path as far as it can go before backtracking to explore the next unexplored branch. It commits to a direction immediately, always choosing depth over breadth.

DFS is most naturally expressed with recursion, where the call stack remembers where to backtrack to. It can also be implemented iteratively with an explicit stack.

### How does recursive DFS work?

The recursive form marks the current node as visited, processes it, then recurses into each unvisited neighbour. When a recursive call returns, control naturally resumes at the previous node, which is the backtracking step.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class FraudTrailTracer {

    private Map<String, List<String>> transferGraph;
    private Set<String> visited;

    public FraudTrailTracer(Map<String, List<String>> transferGraph) {
        this.transferGraph = transferGraph;
        this.visited = new HashSet<>();
    }

    public void traceRecursive(String account) {
        if (visited.contains(account)) return;
        visited.add(account);
        System.out.println("Investigating account: " + account);

        for (String next : transferGraph.getOrDefault(account, Collections.emptyList())) {
            traceRecursive(next);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, List<String>> graph = new HashMap<>();
        graph.put("Rajesh", Arrays.asList("Priya", "Amit"));
        graph.put("Priya", Arrays.asList("Deepak"));
        graph.put("Amit", Arrays.asList("Sonal"));
        graph.put("Deepak", Collections.emptyList());
        graph.put("Sonal", Collections.emptyList());

        FraudTrailTracer tracer = new FraudTrailTracer(graph);
        tracer.traceRecursive("Rajesh");
    }
}
```

