### How does iterative DFS work?

The iterative version replaces the call stack with an explicit stack. Push the source, then repeatedly pop a node, process it if unvisited, and push its neighbours.

```java
public void traceIterative(String startAccount) {
    Stack<String> stack = new Stack<>();
    stack.push(startAccount);

    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
        String account = stack.pop();
        if (visited.contains(account)) continue;
        visited.add(account);
        System.out.println("Investigating account: " + account);

        List<String> neighbours = transferGraph.getOrDefault(account, Collections.emptyList());
        for (int i = neighbours.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            stack.push(neighbours.get(i));
        }
    }
}
// Note: pushing in reverse order preserves left-to-right processing
```

### How is DFS different from BFS?

| Aspect | DFS | BFS |
|---|---|---|
| Data structure | Stack (or recursion) | Queue |
| Exploration | Goes deep first | Goes wide first |
| Shortest path | No guarantee | Guarantees shortest (unweighted) |
| Memory | O(depth) | O(width of widest level) |
| Cycle detection | Back-edge on recursion stack | Not directly |
| Topological sort | Yes, via finish order | Yes, via Kahn's algorithm |
| Use case | Path existence, cycles, components | Shortest path, level order |

```java
// BFS uses a queue instead of a stack
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer(startAccount);
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    String account = queue.poll();
    if (visited.contains(account)) continue;
    visited.add(account);
    for (String next : transferGraph.getOrDefault(account, Collections.emptyList())) {
        queue.offer(next);
    }
}
```

### What is DFS used for?

DFS has several important applications beyond simple traversal.

- **Cycle detection** – a back edge to a node on the current recursion stack indicates a cycle.
- **Topological sort** – record nodes in reverse finish order for dependency ordering.
- **Connected components** – run DFS from each unvisited node; each run discovers one component.
- **Path finding** – DFS naturally finds any path between two nodes (not necessarily shortest).
- **Strongly connected components** – Tarjan's and Kosaraju's algorithms use DFS.

```java
// Cycle detection: back edge to node on recursion stack
public boolean hasCycle(String account, Set<String> onStack) {
    visited.add(account);
    onStack.add(account);
    for (String next : transferGraph.getOrDefault(account, Collections.emptyList())) {
        if (onStack.contains(next)) return true; // cycle: money loops back
        if (!visited.contains(next) && hasCycle(next, onStack)) return true;
    }
    onStack.remove(account);
    return false;
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Disconnected graph** – DFS from one node does not visit all nodes; run DFS from every unvisited node.
- **Self-loop** – a node with an edge to itself; the visited check prevents infinite recursion.
- **Empty graph** – no nodes to visit; handle gracefully.
- **Very deep graph** – recursive DFS overflows the call stack; use iterative version.

```java
// Disconnected graph: must start DFS from each unvisited node
for (String account : transferGraph.keySet()) {
    if (!visited.contains(account)) {
        traceRecursive(account);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the visited set in a graph, causing infinite loops on cycles.

```java
// WRONG: no visited check in a graph with cycles
public void dfsBuggy(String account) {
    System.out.println(account);
    for (String next : transferGraph.getOrDefault(account, Collections.emptyList())) {
        dfsBuggy(next); // infinite loop if cycle exists
    }
}

// CORRECT: check visited before recursing
public void dfsCorrect(String account) {
    if (visited.contains(account)) return;
    visited.add(account);
    for (String next : transferGraph.getOrDefault(account, Collections.emptyList())) {
        dfsCorrect(next);
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

DFS visits every vertex once and examines every edge once, giving O(V + E) time. Space is O(V) for the visited set plus O(h) for the recursion stack (or O(V) in the worst case of a degenerate graph). For a banking transaction graph with millions of accounts, the iterative version is preferred to avoid stack overflow.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know the distinction between tree edges, back edges, forward edges, and cross edges in a DFS forest. Back edges indicate cycles, and the DFS finish time ordering produces a topological sort. They should also understand that DFS memory is proportional to depth, making it lighter than BFS for wide graphs but heavier for deep narrow ones.
